s\" from \"threshold contingency.\" So the full regime set is {A invasion, B off, C constant-matched, C2 bursty-random}, giving a 2\u00d74 grid if compute allows; minimum viable is 2\u00d73 (A, B, C).\n- Threshold manipulation: \u03b8 \u2208 {low, high} as a crossed sensitivity factor, to test whether effects depend on when the invasion arrives relative to the agent's skill trajectory.\n\n=== 5. PROCEDURE ===\nPhase 0: implementation + smoke tests (toggle verification, trigger verification, seed-reproducibility check: same seed \u2192 same event schedule).\nPhase 1: pilots (N=20 scripted-agent episodes per regime) \u2192 calibration of C/C2, estimate survival-time variance for power reasoning.\nPhase 2: training (if trained-agent track chosen) with equal budgets.\nPhase 3: evaluation: per seed, M=50 evaluation episodes, deterministic evaluation protocol (\u03b5=0, fixed spawn layout sampled from a seeded pool), episode truncation at T_max with censoring flag.\nInteraction budget: explicit design parameter B_int (episodes\u00d7steps per agent). The number \"60\" seen in my orchestration metadata is NOT adopted as a fact; the design is parameterized, with a recommended minimum and a feasibility note below.\n\n=== 6. METRICS ===\nPrimary: survival duration in environment steps to death (censored at T_max).\nSecondary: time-to-threshold-crossing (mediator), score trajectory (rate of score accumulation \u2014 to detect \"hierarchy just scores differently\"), damage taken before/after crossing (within-episode contrast), for hierarchical agents: option-usage statistics (pre-invasion preparatory option frequency \u2014 mechanistic evidence of anticipation).\n\n=== 7. ANALYSIS PLAN ===\n- Kaplan\u2013Meier survival curves per cell, log-rank tests pairwise.\n- Pre-specified primary test: Cox proportional-hazards model with predictors hierarchy, regime, hierarchy\u00d7regime interaction; stratified by seed; survival times from different seeds treated as clustered (robust SEs).\n- Mediation: does hierarchy affect survival *through* earlier/later threshold-crossing? Formal mediation decomposition (natural indirect effect via bootstrap).\n- Multiple comparisons: Holm correction over the pre-specified interaction contrasts.\n- Power reasoning (no runs): with heavily right-skewed/censored survival data, interaction tests need ~4\u00d7 the sample of main effects; heuristic target: \u226520 seeds \u00d7 50 eval episodes per cell for the trained track (\u22485,000 episodes/cell), which typically resolves hazard-ratio interactions \u22651.3\u20131.5 at \u03b1=0.05 under CV of survival times typical for survival games; final seed count adjusted after Phase-1 variance estimates. This is argued, not claimed as measured.\n- Sensitivity analyses (pre-specified): \u03b8 low/high; one-shot vs recurring invasions; invasion intensity multiplier {0.5\u00d7, 1\u00d7, 2\u00d7}; exclusion of early deaths (< some floor) to check the effect isn't just \"invasion instantly kills weak agents.\"\n\n=== 8. FEASIBILITY (qualitative, no compute claimed) ===\n- Minecraft track: engineering cost concentrated in the datapack trigger + observation/action plumbing; moderate risk. Fallback simulator: low risk, weaker external validity. Rule-based track: minimal compute; trained track: the dominant cost \u2014 bounded by choosing a small map, short T_max, and modest agent sizes; exact figures are design targets, not verified runtimes.\n- Everything is executable in principle on CPU-only infrastructure at small scale; larger seed counts would need scaled compute (flagged as the main feasibility risk of the trained track).\n\n=== 9. ASSUMPTIONS (declared) vs FIXED REQUIREMENTS ===\nFixed by researcher: mechanic must be built-in and toggleable; structural definition of hierarchy; KM/Cox + interaction acceptable; sensitivity analysis over trigger mechanics required; explicit calibration spec for the constant-threat control; no execution.\nMy assumptions (not facts): E1 environment choice (Minecraft primary, abstract fallback); E2 seedability; E3 parameter-count matching suffices for comparability; E4 damage-rate + total-damage matching is a fair operationalization of \"equivalent difficulty\"; E5 trained-agent track is preferred over rule-based for external validity (rule-based track retained as documented alternative).\n\n=== 10. THREATS TO VALIDITY (honest limits) ===\n- If the invasion's difficulty is dominated by total damage rather than temporal structure, conditions A and C converge \u2014 that itself is the interpretable finding, not a failure.\n- Hierarchical agents may simply train slower; equal-budget matching mitigates but cannot eliminate this (report learning curves).\n- Minecraft's own stochasticity (mob spawns, terrain) adds variance; seeded world generation and fixed layout pools mitigate.\n- actor_step_budget=60 appears only in my orchestration metadata and is explicitly NOT confirmed by the researcher's brief; it is carried in this design only as an unverified environment note, with the actual interaction budget treated as a design parameter (see \u00a75).\n\nNo experiment was run; all numbers above are design targets or reasoned heuristics, none is an outcome.", "evidence": "Repository observations: /workspace contains only task.json (verified via find/ls: one 482-byte file). task.json includes question, context (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"), and track.md text repeating the same. No environment specs, no agent code, no datasets \u2014 hence the environment is a declared assumption (E1), confirmed by the researcher. The \"actor_step_budget: 60\" string comes from the task-environment metadata JSON in my inputs (alongside network: disabled, gpu_enabled: false), not from the researcher's brief; per their instruction it is reported with its source and demoted to an unverified note / design parameter. Python 3.14.4 present; no RL libraries installed (irrelevant for design-only). No command